<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Perplexity Computation</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a response sequence <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=(r_{1},\dots,r_{|R|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=(r_{1},\dots,r_{|R|})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, the perplexity under model <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on context <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{PPL}_{f_{s}}(c,R_{L}\mid u)=\exp\!\left(-\frac{1}{|R_{L}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|R_{L}|}\log P_{f_{s}}(r_{t}\mid u,c,r_{&lt;t})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>PPL</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PPL}_{f_{s}}(c,R_{L}\mid u)=\exp\!\left(-\frac{1}{|R_{L}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|R_{L}|}\log P_{f_{s}}(r_{t}\mid u,c,r_{&lt;t})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{&lt;t}=(r_{1},\dots,r_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{&lt;t}=(r_{1},\dots,r_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the preceding tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Connective Data Construction</h3>

<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.1 </span>Connective Definition</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support low-risk early speech and enable rapid response onset, we characterize the connectives used in this work by two essential properties. First, <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positional specificity</span>. A connective must appear turn-initially at the boundary between two consecutive speakers, explicitly linking Speaker B’s response (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) to the immediately preceding utterance produced by Speaker A (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>). We therefore focus on turn-transition connectives that form an <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>–[connective]–<math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> structure, excluding sentence-internal or mid-utterance discourse markers.
Second, <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">non–truth-conditional meaning</span>. The connective must not contribute to the propositional content of <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Removing the connective preserves the semantic payload of the response and only affects how the discourse relation between the two turns is interpreted. This property makes such connectives suitable as low-risk prefixes that can be emitted before substantive reasoning is complete. Based on these two properties, we ground our definition in Fraser’s theory of pragmatic markers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fraser, 2009</a>]</cite> and selectively retain discourse markers that meet these constraints. Concrete categories and examples are provided in the Table  <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.2.1 Connective Definition ‣ A.2 Connective Data Construction ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Discourse-Aware Dual-Track Streaming Response for Low-Latency Spoken Dialogue Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;">
<span id="A1.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:71.1pt;">
