<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Curriculum-Learning-Based Discourse Continuity Enhancement.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement this module through three training stages. <span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter-efficient adaptation</span> fine-tunes the lightweight model with LoRA (<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}64</annotation></semantics></math>; learning rate <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{\times}10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{\times}10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>) to jointly generate turn-transition connectives and full responses. <span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Curriculum scheduling</span> organizes samples into four stages based on the empirical distribution of audio chunks (500 ms each), reflecting response difficulty under partial input; training proceeds from harder to easier conditions, with epochs allocated in reverse order (5/3/3/2). The weighting parameters <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{con}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{con}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{coh}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>coh</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{coh}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{prior}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>prior</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{prior}}</annotation></semantics></math> are set to 1.0, 0.5, and 0.1, respectively. <span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confidence-based pre-filling</span> bridges training and inference by emitting early connectives whose confidence exceeds dataset-specific thresholds (<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}0.45</annotation></semantics></math> for SD-Eval, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.15</annotation></semantics></math> for SpokenNativQA), with the pre-filling horizon capped at <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\max}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\max}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>. We select the top 5-10 candidate connectives as <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in the step <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and found that 5 achieved the best performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For text quality, we focus on logical consistency and discourse coherence. Logical Consistency measures whether the generated response aligns with the dialogue context without contradictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>]</cite>. discourse coherence evaluates discourse-level organization and fluency across sentences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grosz and Sidner, 1986</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stede, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fabbri et al., 2021</a>]</cite>. Following recent work on automatic evaluation, we adopt an LLM-as-a-judge protocol using the G-Eval framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>]</cite>, which has been shown effective for assessing discourse quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
